00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
hello i would like to thank the  organizers of the napb meeting and this  session for the opportunity to share a  few highlights of our research and  field-based either put phenotyping over  the past 10 years and convey our future  research directions  the path from genotype to terminal  phenotype  such as yield covers a large range of  spatial and temporal scales  the diverse set of processes operating  at and across the intervening scales can  have subtle effects on the terminal  phenotype  however they also govern a large set of  endophenotypes  or intermediate phenotypes including  properties like protein and metabolite  levels  or physiological and morphological  attributes of the plant that can be  modeled  furthermore interactions between  processes within and across scales  can lead to emergent behavior  my talk today will focus on how we are  merging the fields of plant biology  and robotics  to collect early season intermediate  phenotypes or into phenotypes for  building models that are highly  predicted of terminal phenotypes  at the end of season  radical collaborations were essential to  advancing dna sequencing technology  to where it now costs about a hundred  dollars to resequence a human genome  this has resulted in a deluge of  low-cost genomics data  in contrast phenotyping is a 10 000 year  old challenge that started with the  visual assessment of plants for  desirable attributes at the time of  plant domestication  phenomics technologies have only just  begun to evolve more rapidly  and phenomics data are typically more  expensive to collect than genomics data  on a large scale  this has caused the massive imbalance in  the volume of data collected  radical collaborations among plant  biologists  engineers and computer scientists are  needed to bring the lab to the field  allowing multi-scale data to be  collected  the concept of field-based  high-throughput phenotyping  fbhtp  has been advocated by those of us in the  plant-breeding community  to reduce human effort  collect higher amounts of data  evaluate large population sizes at many  time points in a growing season  decrease subjectivity when scoring  plants  and diminish phenotyping costs over time  on the left is an example of a more  classical physiological phenotyping  approach  with an instrument that collects highly  detailed data but does not scale well to  large plant populations  on the right is an example of a  phenotyping system with greater  throughput for non-contact simultaneous  measurements of multiple canopy  phenotypes in the field environment  stimulated by the need for better  phenotyping in the field i started  collaborating in 2010 with agricultural  engineers at the university of arizona  while i was a research geneticist  working for the usda ars in maricopa  arizona  our design field-based high-speed  phenotyping system  shown in this icon  was used to study the genetic basis of  physiological traits and their  relationship to lint yield in a cotton  recombinant inbred lawn population  the field based high put throughput  phenotyping system  could scan our field trial of nearly 500  plots in less than one hour  furthermore we use this rapid  phenotyping platform to monitor changes  in three physiological phenotypes and  multiple time points within a day  and multiple days across the growing  season  and these three canopy traits  were temperature normalized difference  vegetation index or ndvi and height  with canopy temperature as an example  these data were used to map qtl  expression patterns across  cotton plant development  as an intermediate phenotype  these canopy temperature data were also  found to have a moderately strong  correlation with lint yield at peak  flowering  despite this progress  i was still driven by the need to better  dissect  the components of lint yield  in collaboration with andy french and  allison thompson at usdars maricopa  arizona we demonstrated the potential of  light detection and ranging or lidar  sometimes called laser scanning for  phenotyping defoliated cotton plants  the field based high throughput  phenotyping system fit it with a lidar  unit  scanned the entire field  allowing us to extract lidar data  on a per plot basis  and a per plant basis  in this point cloud visualization of a  single extracted cotton plant  a euclidean clustering approach was used  to count the number of cotton bowls with  reasonable accuracy  this was an initial indication to us  that optical phenotyping could be used  to better understand yield components  ground vehicles and lidar units have  evolved since our early work in cotton  in a collaboration with ed buckler led  by joe gage a postdoc in his lab the  tara cinch's sub-canopy rover from  earthsense fitted with a lidar unit  was used to characterize the whole plant  architecture of maize plants in the  field  joe used the computational workflow to  compress  the 3d lidar point cloud data into 2d  density maps  which were then analyzed with two  dimensionality reduction techniques  principle component analysis and an auto  encoder  the extracted latent space phenotypes  lsps  were used to predict manually measured  phenotypes by partial least squares  regression  the lsps contained enough plant  architecture information  to predict  architectural and non-architectural  traits  joe is continuing this work to map  g by time and growth curve parameters in  maze hybrids that are part of the  genomes to fields initiative  we now shift our attention from the  ground to the sky  in the past few years we have assessed  the potential use of unoccupied aerial  vehicles uavs  as efficient co-workers for precisely  monitoring the incidence  and severity of northern leaf blight a  maze foliar pathogen  the aerial system that we used at the  time consisted as shown here of a dji  matrice 600 and a sony alpha a6000  camera with a zoom lens  the large size nlb lesions as seen in  the image on the right  makes the disease an attractive  candidate  for image-based phenotyping  in a collaborative study involving hod  lipson and his research group at  columbia university and rebecca nelson  in her research group at cornell  university  harvey wu an undergraduate student in  hod's lab use a convolutional neural  network or cnn model pre-trained on  imagenet  to transfer learn rather than training  from scratch  for a presence absence detection of nlb  lesions  the cnn model created interpretable heat  maps as shown here on the right  of the original images  showing the locations of putative  lesions  the cnn model achieved 95 percent  accuracy on a separate test test and  resolution was achieved at the sub-leaf  scale  beyond incidence  breeders and pathologists want to  measure disease severity  which is a quantitative attribute  thus we attempted to estimate the number  size and location of nlb lesions  ethan stewart a former postdoc in my lab  trained a mascar cnn model  to segment nlb disease lesions in uav  images  this deep learning model approach for  instance segmentation accurately  detected  and segmented individual lesions in a  holdout test set  the mean intersect over union iou  between the ground truth and predicted  lesions was 0.73  with an average precision of 0.96  at an intersection over union threshold  of 0.50  in these images shown here the ground  truth is marked with white polygons  and predicted lesion instances marked in  magenta  allowing you to visualize the high  degree of intersection  one note  is that due to variation in plan height  and image resizing during data set  preparation the scale here is variable  between images and provided for general  guidance only  i would like to now highlight our  efforts in wheat and cassava  first in wheat  in a collaboration with simmet a former  phd student co-advised by mark sorel's  and me margaret kraus  demonstrated the use of hyperspectral  reflectance derived relationship  matrices  for the prediction of grain yield in  cement wheat in mexico  the aerial hyperspectral phenotyping  approach can monitor physiological  changes resulting from plant growth  stage and the environment  therefore hyperspectral  reflectance-derived relationship  matrices can capture both genetic  manifest and  genotype by environment interactions  this contrasts with pedigree and genomic  relationship matrices  which are static  shown in this figure are the single  environment prediction accuracies of  wheat yield  across four site years two environments  and two breeding cycles with g as the  genomic relationship matrix  the a matrix derived from pedigree  and h as the hyperspectral derived  relationship matrices  when considering the growth stage at the  time of phenotyping  prediction accuracies were highest at  the heading and grain fill stages  overall  the hyperspectral base accuracies are  similar to  or slightly higher than  genomic and pedigree based  relationship matrices  i encourage you all to look at the  results of multi-environmental  prediction and correcting for days to  heading in margaret's g3 paper that was  published in  2019  in our lab we have also worked on the  use of low-cost  handheld spectrometers for phenotyping  quality traits and planned breeding  programs jenna hershberger a recent phd  graduate from our lab  led a collaboration with iita in nigeria  to predict cassava root dry matter with  the handheld scio spectrometer shown  here on the left jenna found cassava  root dry matter content to be highly  predictable within 10 field trials  as shown in the figure on the right  mean partial least squares regression  model performance range from an rs  square of 0.62 to 0.89  for within trial predictions  which is within the range typically  achieved with laboratory grade  spectrometers  a pre-print of bioarchive is available  on jenna's work if you would like to  learn more  in general technology has largely  limited us to phenotyping macro-scale  traits  and indirectly measuring those traits at  best  working with piers gian  a phd student in abe strokes lab at  cornell university  in washington liu a former postdoc in my  lab and others at cornell  we developed swelling shrinking  nanoparticles with fluorescent reporters  to rapidly detect water status inside a  plant  aquadus as we call it shrinks with  decreasing water potential and hence  there is increased interaction between  two fluorophores we measure this  interaction in terms of fret efficiency  aqua dust which resides within the  apoplastic region  allows for in-situ minimally invasive  measurements of water status as water  potential in local physiologically  relevant micro environments of leaves  using an external sensor  this work represents a major step  towards high throughput phenotyping of  water potential  ultimately we can couple water potential  variation with biological and physical  processes  to accelerate our understanding of the  factors  that are critical for improving drought  tolerance and crops  this work was recently published in  panas this year  what is the next step for the use of  aerial and ground systems in planned  phenotyping  through two recently funded nsf  proposals we are collaborating with  plant biologists  engineers and modelers at cornell  to collect phenotypic data with robots  operating at the canopy understory and  below ground levels  rob shepard's robotics group will lead  the development of a one to two foot  worm-like robot  that emulates how a board drills into  the ground  combined with a wave-like motion that  copies how worms move through soil  by quantifying root features soil  properties  metabolites and water that includes the  use of aqua dust we hope to use  predictive models to combine the  multi-scale below  and above ground characteristics as  intermediate features  to predict such traits as grain yield  and stress tolerance  agriculture in the 20th century has  relied on chemistry and qualitative  patterns  i think that agriculture in the 21st  century will be the century of robotics  and pinpointing the causal factors  driving those correlative patterns  it will then better allow us to model  what we know and if needed predict what  we don't know  our multi-institution group of  collaborators comprising the proposed  center for research on programmable  plants crops  is led by susan mccooch at cornell  university  notably crops grew out of the cornell  institute for digital agriculture ceda  within the crops project we share a  vision of developing programmable plants  with genome-encoded gene switches  sensors and reporters with digital  readouts  serving as endophenotypes that can be  read into software and acted upon by  robots  we collectively call this the internet  of living things  how could we begin harnessing the  genetic power of programmable plant  populations  in one example the crops internet of  living things could be used to perform  intensive multi-scale measurements of  endo phenotypes across a training  population  of genetically diverse programmable  plants  this will serve as the genetic framework  for the use of genomic prediction models  to predict endo phenotypes such as  protein abundance in developing plant  tissues for hundreds to thousands of  untested genotypes  it will further allow us to predict the  function and activity of gene circuits  and untested genetic  backgrounds would like to thank all  those that have contributed to this work  and the funding agencies that have  provided support  especially columbia university  university illinois urbana champaign  earth sense university of arizona  nextgen cassava usa lark  and all of our collaborators at cornell  university  i'm happy to take your questions at this  time thank you very much for listening

